    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, PARTLY-STALE] 2026-11-01 review @100b6fbb6e: on a Kafka + Redis fixture, both event_publishes edges had channel=='topic' ('orders'/'orders', 'alerts'/'alerts'). message_queue.py:577-593 passes channel=join.channel and topic=join.value. The 103/103 self-corpus count is stale: those edges came from test files (drained by WI-nitob), and today's packages/ self-analysis has 0 event_publishes edges. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\n**Defect.** On event-publish edges, `meta.channel` duplicates `meta.topic` exactly — two keys carrying the same string.\n\n**Evidence.** The producer writes both channel and topic onto event_publishes edges, and the two values are identical on every such edge: channel != topic on 0 of 103 (identical 103/103).\n\n**Contract.** Two distinct metadata keys should carry distinct information; one of the redundant keys should be dropped.\n\n**Consequence.** A consumer reading message-publish metadata sees two keys that always agree, with no documented reason for both; a consumer that branches on a perceived difference between them will never see one and may assume the redundancy is meaningful."  # 508c
